findings.description:
The client "reconstructs by accumulating deltas onto its last-known positions." 300-arch (line 52) permits backpressure drops on STATE_UPDATE up to 5% under saturation. One dropped or reordered delta silently corrupts that client's positions until the next full-position event. The doc defines those events only as "teleportation, spawn." It specifies no keyframe for:
- a dropped STATE_UPDATE;
- a client connecting or reconnecting mid-stream;
- an agent re-entering a client's frustum. Tier 3 culling makes agents leave and re-enter each client's set, and each re-entry has no baseline.

The per-client baseline is also unaccounted for: 2M float32 3-D positions is 24 MB per client.

The project's review rule (`review-code.md`, Failure-Mode Enumeration) requires each new I/O path (binary WebSocket, camera channel, delta stream) to name its hang, timeout, and mid-send disconnect modes and make each one observable. The doc does this for none of them. The "large-delta fallback" needs a counter, or it is the silent fallback CLAUDE.md forbids.
